// How a page lays its boxes out, every page the same way.

import { Space } from "tokens.slint";

// The row of boxes on a device's page, under its stage (or under the page's head
// where it has none), beside the Profiles column: the tab's boxes left to right,
// Space.gap apart. Each box is at least its least width (its `min-width`, and
// its `preferred-width` the same) and grows from it as the row's other boxes do,
// so every line of boxes reaches the row's right edge; a box that can't use much
// more room says so with a `max-width`, and the rest goes to its neighbors. A box
// that doesn't fit beside the one before it starts a line under them. The boxes
// in a line are as tall as each other: as tall as the tallest needs (its
// `preferred-height`), at most the room left in the window (`max-height`), a box
// that holds more scrolling inside itself; the row as a whole at least as tall as
// the Profiles column beside it (the page sets its height so), its one line then
// that tall. A box whose pieces are laid out in Rust says how wide the row made
// it, so Rust lays them out to its width (CellBox).
export component BoxRow inherits FlexboxLayout {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    spacing: Space.gap;
    alignment: stretch;
    cross-axis-alignment: stretch;
}
